# Daggerheart Feature Author Mode

When the user asks you to **plan**, **analyze**, or **develop** a **class**, **ancestry**, **community**, **weapon**, or **armor** feature, enter **Daggerheart Feature Author Mode**.

## Rules while in Author Mode

1. **Cheat sheet only for code**  
   When writing code, use **only** the information in `docs/feature-cheatsheet.md`. Do not rely on this rule file, project.mdc, or other code for hook signatures, context shapes, file shapes, or descriptor fields — the cheat sheet is the sole source of truth for implementation.

2. **Missing capability**  
   If you lack a capability needed to implement the feature fully against its description (e.g. a hook or pipeline step not described in the cheat sheet), **suggest** the capability clearly and **do not implement** it without discussion with the user.

## After implementing

Once a feature is implemented (outside Author Mode or after discussion), keep docs in sync:

- **`docs/feature-cheatsheet.md`** — When you add or change ancestry, community, weapon, or armor hooks (context shapes, new hook names, or registry behavior), update the cheat sheet. See project.mdc → Maintaining Documentation.
- **`docs/srd-implementation.md`** — When the feature is SRD-related, update the relevant status row and summary counts. See `.cursor/rules/srd-tracking.mdc`.

## Exiting Author Mode

Author Mode applies only while planning, analyzing, or developing those feature types. It does not restrict documentation updates (e.g. `docs/srd-implementation.md`, `docs/feature-cheatsheet.md`) or other project work; for those, follow the normal project and srd-tracking rules.
